8. Farm and Investment Opportunity Data
A farm profile and a fund-seeking opportunity should be separate records. A farm may exist on the platform without seeking investment.
• Farm profile: farm name, owner/contact, location (with privacy controls), farm type, crops/livestock, operating history, scale/capacity, photos, certifications where applicable, and verification status.
• Opportunity: project title, purpose, amount sought, intended use of funds, proposed investment/participation structure, duration, milestones, financial assumptions, key risks, exit/repayment terms if applicable, fees, eligibility, documents, and update frequency.
• Evidence: registration documents where applicable, ownership/lease evidence, relevant permits, historical records, budgets, quotations, field photos, and other documents required by the review policy.
• Disclosure: past performance is not a guarantee of future results; all material risks and fees must be presented clearly.
• Visibility: only approved, current opportunities are publicly discoverable; draft and rejected records remain private.
9. Payments and Financial Controls
The platform must distinguish between (a) service fees paid to professionals or the platform, (b) organisation project payments, and (c) any funds contributed to farm opportunities. These flows may require different contracts, payment arrangements, reconciliation, and regulatory treatment.
• Use a reputable payment service provider that supports the intended Nigerian transaction types and required compliance controls.
• Do not store raw card details; use provider-hosted or tokenised payment flows.
• Record currency, amount, fee breakdown, transaction reference, payer/payee, timestamps, status, refunds, and reconciliation results.
• Show clear statuses such as initiated, pending, successful, failed, refunded, or under review.
• Provide downloadable or emailed receipts for completed transactions.
• Use role-based access and maker-checker approval for sensitive refunds, manual adjustments, or disbursement instructions.
• Investment funds must not be pooled, held, advertised, or disbursed by the platform unless the relevant legal structure, licensed partners, and approvals permit it.
• Define failed-payment handling, duplicate callbacks, chargebacks, refund rules, settlement delays, and dispute escalation.
