14. Legal, Regulatory, and Trust Considerations
Because the platform may present farm investment opportunities and facilitate payments, the product owner must obtain Nigerian legal and regulatory advice before enabling investment solicitation, collecting investment funds, promising returns, or arranging disbursements. The correct treatment depends on the actual product structure and how the platform operates.
• Have counsel assess applicable Nigerian securities/investment, company, consumer-protection, payment, AML/KYC, data-protection, tax, and other requirements.
• Use a qualified payment or custody partner where required; confirm in writing which party receives, safeguards, settles, refunds, and disburses funds.
• Provide clear risk disclosures and avoid language implying guaranteed returns or risk-free investment.
• Define investor eligibility, identity/business checks, source-of-funds checks where required, complaints handling, conflicts of interest, and record retention.
• Publish privacy, terms of use, service terms, refund/cancellation rules, acceptable-use policy, and investment risk disclosures reviewed by counsel.
• Do not treat a farm profile as proof of ownership, project legitimacy, financial performance, or investment suitability.
• Make verification scope explicit: indicate what was checked, by whom, and when.
